WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.718
Every other screen in this Orrery refers to physics.

00:00:03.718 --> 00:00:05.005
Hohmann transfers.

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:06.292
Lambert solutions.

00:00:06.292 --> 00:00:06.935
Vis-viva.

00:00:06.935 --> 00:00:08.437
Free-return geometry.

00:00:08.437 --> 00:00:10.081
Tsiolkovsky's equation.

00:00:10.081 --> 00:00:10.653
C three.

00:00:10.653 --> 00:00:11.225
Delta-V.

00:00:11.225 --> 00:00:12.011
V-infinity.

00:00:12.011 --> 00:00:20.734
This screen is where those terms get explained — with diagrams, with worked-out math, and with the missions that use them.

00:00:20.734 --> 00:00:24.952
The encyclopedia is organized into ten tabs across the top.

00:00:24.952 --> 00:00:25.453
Orbits.

00:00:25.453 --> 00:00:26.168
Maneuvers.

00:00:26.168 --> 00:00:26.954
Propulsion.

00:00:26.954 --> 00:00:27.598
Re-entry.

00:00:27.598 --> 00:00:28.527
Solar System.

00:00:28.527 --> 00:00:28.956
Stars.

00:00:28.956 --> 00:00:29.886
Astrophysics.

00:00:29.886 --> 00:00:30.744
Instruments.

00:00:30.744 --> 00:00:31.673
Life support.

00:00:31.673 --> 00:00:32.459
Operations.

00:00:32.459 --> 00:00:37.393
Each tab opens to a list of sections — eighty-five sections in total.

00:00:37.393 --> 00:00:39.037
Click any section card.

00:00:39.037 --> 00:00:46.902
The full article opens with worked math rendered by KaTeX, hand-drawn SVG diagrams, and links to read further.

00:00:46.902 --> 00:01:06.921
Where a section explains a phenomenon you can see in action elsewhere in Orrery, a chip at the top of the article links straight to it — Hohmann transfer to the Fly screen, free-return to the Plan screen, vis-viva to the Explore screen with the orbital-velocity overlay turned on.

00:01:06.921 --> 00:01:11.854
Notice the small question-mark chips throughout the rest of the site.

00:01:11.854 --> 00:01:16.501
Each one is a hyperlink directly into a specific Science section.

00:01:16.501 --> 00:01:20.434
Whenever you see one and don't know the term, click it.

00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:23.365
The encyclopedia opens at the right page.

00:01:23.365 --> 00:01:24.438
Try the search.

00:01:24.438 --> 00:01:28.370
Press Cmd-K — or Ctrl-K on a Windows or Linux keyboard.

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:29.943
A search dialog opens.

00:01:29.943 --> 00:01:30.944
Type anything.

00:01:30.944 --> 00:01:39.023
The full text of all eighty-five sections is searchable, and results jump you directly to the matching paragraph.

00:01:39.023 --> 00:01:41.883
Seventy-one diagrams are hand-drawn SVG.

00:01:41.883 --> 00:01:54.824
Each one was authored from a primary source — a textbook, a paper, a NASA technical memo — and the source files for the SVGs are committed to the same repository as the site itself.

00:01:54.824 --> 00:01:58.255
Click any diagram for the credit and the source.

00:01:58.255 --> 00:02:00.329
Every formula has a citation.

00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:02.545
Every constant has a reference.

00:02:02.545 --> 00:02:05.548
Every diagram has an author and a license.

00:02:05.548 --> 00:02:06.835
This isn't a wiki.

00:02:06.835 --> 00:02:13.127
It's a working reference written specifically to make the rest of Orrery understandable.

00:02:13.127 --> 00:02:13.913
Pick a tab.

00:02:13.913 --> 00:02:14.986
Pick a section.

00:02:14.986 --> 00:02:15.343
Read.

